      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the midpoint of segment \\( s_1 \\) and then apply the translation to this midpoint.\n\nThe midpoint \\( M \\) of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\\[\nM = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\), we calculate the midpoint of \\( s_1 \\) as follows:\n\\[\nM = \\left( \\frac{4 + (-8)}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{4 - 8}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2} \\right) = (-2, 3)\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the translation of 2 units to the right and 3 units up to the midpoint of \\( s_1 \\). This translates the x-coordinate by 2 and the y-coordinate by 3:\n\\[\n(-2 + 2, 3 + 3) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 6)}\n\\]",
