      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) after it is translated from segment \\( s_1 \\), we first need to determine the midpoint of \\( s_1 \\) and then apply the translation to that midpoint.\n\nThe endpoints of \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\). The formula for the midpoint \\((x_m, y_m)\\) of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\\[\n(x_m, y_m) = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting the coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_1 \\):\n\\[\n(x_m, y_m) = \\left( \\frac{4 + (-8)}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2} \\right) = (-2, 3)\n\\]\nSo, the midpoint of \\( s_1 \\) is \\((-2, 3)\\).\n\nNext, we apply the translation to the midpoint. The translation is 2 units to the right and 3 units up. This means we add 2 to the \\( x \\)-coordinate and 3 to the \\( y \\)-coordinate of the midpoint:\n\\[\n(x_m', y_m') = (-2 + 2, 3 + 3) = (0, 6)\n\\]\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0,6)}\\).",
